Why `classical` instead of `[DecidableEq ...]` in `IsCentral_basechange`
=======================================================================
(file: CMAbelianVarieties/CMTypes/Algebra.lean)

The warning
-----------
The lemma used to have the hypothesis

    [DecidableEq (Basis.ofVectorSpaceIndex K F)]

and Mathlib's linter `linter.unusedDecidableInType` complained:

    `IsCentral_basechange` does not use the following hypothesis in its type:
      • [DecidableEq ↑(Basis.ofVectorSpaceIndex K F)]
    Consider removing this hypothesis and using `classical` in the proof instead.

The statement `IsCentral F (F ⊗[K] R)` is a `Prop` and has nothing to do with
deciding equality. The hypothesis was only used inside the proof.

Why the proof needs decidability
--------------------------------
The definition

    let x (i : I) : K := if hi : i ∈ b.support then (this i hi).choose else 0

uses a dependent `if`. To build it, Lean needs a `Decidable (i ∈ b.support)`
instance, i.e. a way to decide which branch holds. Lean finds this via
`Finset.decidableMem`, which in turn needs `DecidableEq I`. That is why simply
deleting the hypothesis caused the error

    failed to synthesize Decidable (i ∈ b.support)

(The later `split_ifs` / `intro` errors were knock-on failures from this.)

Two ways to provide decidability
--------------------------------
1. As a hypothesis in the statement (the old version):
   anyone who applies the lemma must supply a `DecidableEq` instance for the
   basis index type, a type with no computable decidable equality. That is a
   strange requirement for a purely mathematical statement, so the linter
   flags it.

2. With `classical` at the start of the proof (the current version):
   inside the proof every proposition is treated as decidable (via the law of
   excluded middle). The statement stays clean and the `if` still works.

The extra change
----------------
With `classical`, the step

    have h : x i = (this i hb).choose := by
      simp [x]
      split_ifs
      rfl

broke. `simp [x]` rewrote the condition `i ∈ b.support` to `b i = 0`, and
`split_ifs` could no longer match it against `hb : i ∈ b.support`, leaving an
impossible branch with goal `0 = Exists.choose ⋯`. Rewriting the `if` directly
with `hb` avoids this:

    have h : x i = (this i hb).choose := by
      simp only [x, hb, dite_true]

(`exact dif_pos hb` also works but is deprecated in this Mathlib version.)

Rule of thumb
-------------
If a `Decidable...` / `DecidableEq ...` hypothesis is only needed in the proof
and not in the statement, remove it and write `classical` at the start of the
proof.
